### What is short polling?

Short polling is the simplest approach. The client sends a normal HTTP request every few seconds, and the server responds immediately. Most responses have nothing new. It is trivial to implement but wasteful at scale.

```java
package com.bank.client;

// Client-side: poll every 5 seconds (simplified)
public class ShortPollingClient {
    public void poll() {
        while (true) {
            TransferStatus status = restTemplate.getForObject(
                    "/api/v1/transfers/TXF-001/status", TransferStatus.class);
            if ("COMPLETED".equals(status.status())) break;
            Thread.sleep(5000);  // wait 5 seconds, try again
        }
    }
}
```

```text
10:00:00  GET /status → { "status": "PENDING" }    ← empty
10:00:05  GET /status → { "status": "PENDING" }    ← empty
10:00:10  GET /status → { "status": "PENDING" }    ← empty
10:00:15  GET /status → { "status": "COMPLETED" }  ← useful!
// 3 out of 4 calls were wasted
```

### What is long polling?

The client sends a request and the server holds it open until data arrives or a timeout. When data arrives, the server responds and the client immediately opens a new request.

```java
package com.bank.controller;

import org.springframework.web.context.request.async.DeferredResult;

@GetMapping("/api/v1/transfers/{id}/updates")
public DeferredResult<TransferStatus> longPoll(@PathVariable String id) {
    DeferredResult<TransferStatus> result = new DeferredResult<>(30000L); // 30s timeout
    updateQueue.register(id, result::setResult); // completed when data arrives
    result.onTimeout(() -> result.setResult(new TransferStatus("PENDING")));
    return result;
}
```

### What is SSE (Server-Sent Events)?

SSE is a true one-way streaming push. The client opens one HTTP connection with `Accept: text/event-stream`, and the server keeps it open and writes events as they occur. The browser's `EventSource` API reconnects automatically.

```java
package com.bank.controller;

import org.springframework.http.MediaType;
import org.springframework.web.servlet.mvc.method.annotation.SseEmitter;

@GetMapping(value = "/api/v1/prices", produces = MediaType.TEXT_EVENT_STREAM_VALUE)
public SseEmitter streamPrices() {
    SseEmitter emitter = new SseEmitter(0L); // no timeout
    priceFeed.subscribe(price ->
            emitter.send(SseEmitter.event()
                    .id(String.valueOf(price.id()))
                    .data(price)));
    return emitter;
}
```

```javascript
// Browser client – 3 lines, auto-reconnect built in:
const es = new EventSource('/api/v1/prices');
es.onmessage = (e) => render(JSON.parse(e.data));
es.onerror = () => console.log('Reconnecting...'); // automatic
```

### What is WebSocket?

WebSocket is full-duplex. It begins as an HTTP request with `Upgrade: websocket`, the server responds 101, and both sides send messages freely with very low per-message overhead.

```java
package com.bank.websocket;

import org.springframework.web.socket.*;
import org.springframework.web.socket.handler.TextWebSocketHandler;

public class TradingHandler extends TextWebSocketHandler {
    @Override
    protected void handleTextMessage(WebSocketSession session, TextMessage message) {
        // Client sent an order → process and send fill back
        Order order = parse(message.getPayload());
        Fill fill = matchEngine.execute(order);
        session.sendMessage(new TextMessage(toJson(fill)));
    }
}
```

### How do the four techniques compare? (comparison table)

| Feature | Short Polling | Long Polling | SSE | WebSocket |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Direction | Client → Server | Client → Server | Server → Client | Both ways |
| Connection | New per poll | Held per update | One persistent | One persistent |
| Wasted calls | Many | Few | None | None |
| Auto-reconnect | N/A | Manual | Built-in | Manual |
| Binary support | No | No | No (text only) | Yes |
| Complexity | Lowest | Low | Medium | Highest |
| Infrastructure | Standard HTTP | Standard HTTP | Standard HTTP | WebSocket-aware |

### When should you use each?

Use the least powerful technique that meets the need. If the server only pushes to the client, SSE is simpler and more robust than WebSocket. If both sides must talk interactively, WebSocket is required. Polling is a fallback for simple cases or environments where streaming is blocked.

```text
Decision tree:
  Is the client sending data back in real time?
    Yes → WebSocket (chat, trading, collaboration)
    No  → Is the server pushing updates?
      Yes → SSE (notifications, tickers, dashboards)
      No  → Short polling or long polling (simple status checks)
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is using WebSocket for a one-way feed like a stock ticker, carrying the complexity of reconnection logic for something SSE handles automatically. Another mistake is short polling without a cap, flooding the server at scale.

```java
// Wrong – WebSocket for a one-way price feed:
// Must implement reconnection, heartbeats, lifecycle management

// Correct – SSE for a one-way price feed:
@GetMapping(value = "/prices", produces = MediaType.TEXT_EVENT_STREAM_VALUE)
public SseEmitter prices() { ... } // auto-reconnect built in
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer matches the technique to the direction and latency of each feature. In banking, notifications and tickers are SSE. A trading terminal or support chat is WebSocket. A rarely changing status that tolerates delay uses polling. If a mostly-one-way feature needs an occasional client action, that action is a normal REST call alongside the SSE stream, keeping the streaming channel simple.
